## Your Task

You are addressing review feedback on PR #226 (feat: add public-holidays box type (Nager.Date)), branch `rfc3-223`, repo `jasoneplumb/infobento.com`.

## Context you must know before starting

The PR received one review round with 4 findings. A commit timestamp postdates the review, but I verified the branch head directly: **none of the 4 findings are fixed.** `packages/renderer/src/boxes/holidays.ts` still returns `Math.max(0, Math.ceil(diffMs / (1000*60*60*24)))`; `packages/core/src/validation.ts:196` still has `countryCode: z.string().min(1, 'Country code is required')`; there is no `holidays` entry in `packages/renderer/src/icons.ts`. Treat all 4 as open.

## Setup

    git fetch origin rfc3-223
    git switch -c tend-226-work origin/rfc3-223

Work happens ONLY inside this worktree. Never run git branch operations from the main repo root.

## The 4 findings

Read them in full yourself first — the inline comments carry `suggestion` blocks and detail this summary omits:

    REPO=jasoneplumb/infobento.com
    gh api --paginate repos/$REPO/pulls/226/comments | jq -r '.[] | select(.user.login=="claude" or .user.login=="claude[bot]") | "\n--- \(.path):\(.line // .original_line) ---\n\(.body)"'
    gh pr view 226 --repo $REPO --json comments --jq '.comments[] | select(.author.login=="claude" or .author.login=="claude[bot]") | .body'

1. **🔴 Bug — `Math.max(0, NaN)` renders "NaN" as hero text** (`packages/renderer/src/boxes/holidays.ts:24`). `Math.max(0, NaN)` is `NaN` in JS — NaN propagates, it is not treated as less-than-zero. A malformed `isoDate` makes `new Date(isoDate + 'T00:00:00').getTime()` NaN, and in `renderHolidaysBox` the `NaN === 0` check is false, so `String(NaN)` → `"NaN"` is drawn as hero text. Reachable today via a crafted `POST /api/render` payload because of finding 3. Add an `isNaN` guard before the return.

2. **🔴 Bug — DST fall-back off-by-one** (same line). `Math.ceil(diffMs / 86_400_000)` is wrong when a DST fall-back falls between today's local midnight and the holiday's local midnight: that night spans 25 wall-clock hours, so `Math.ceil(25/24) = 2` where the correct answer is 1. `Math.round` handles both DST directions correctly. **Add a regression test that actually exercises a DST boundary** — pass an explicit `now` (the function already takes `now: Date = new Date()`) so the test is deterministic and does not depend on the machine's timezone.

3. **🟡 Schema — `HolidayData.date` accepts any string** (`packages/core/src/validation.ts:191`). `date: z.string()` lets `"not-a-date"` or `"2025/12/25"` pass validation, reach the DB, and trigger finding 1. Constrain with `.regex(/^\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2}$/, 'Must be an ISO date (YYYY-MM-DD)')`.

4. **🟡 Security — `countryCode` allows URL path characters** (`packages/core/src/validation.ts:196`). `z.string().min(1)` permits `/`, `.`, `?`. The value is interpolated straight into `…/NextPublicHolidays/${code}` without `encodeURIComponent`, so `GB/../../v2/Other` traverses to an unintended path on `date.nager.at`. The web UI's client-side `maxLength=2` does not protect the API endpoint. Constrain to `/^[A-Z]{2}$/`. **Important interaction:** `fetchNextPublicHoliday` currently calls `.trim().toUpperCase()` on the code — if you enforce uppercase-only at the schema layer, a caller sending `"gb"` now fails validation. Decide deliberately whether to (a) keep `.toUpperCase()` and validate case-insensitively, or (b) enforce uppercase in the schema and update the web form to uppercase before submit. Check `packages/web` for where the country code is set, and make the whole path consistent — do not leave the web UI able to submit a value the API rejects.

5. **ℹ️ Visual gap — `'holidays'` missing from the source-icon map** (`packages/renderer/src/icons.ts`). `drawBoxHeader` silently skips the icon when the entry is `undefined`, so holidays boxes render label text with no leading icon while every other box type has one. Add an icon consistent with the existing set.

**Also check:** mainline has since merged PR #225 `feat(data): add shared SSRF fetch guard for user-supplied URLs`. Look at that guard and decide whether the holidays provider should use it. If a rebase onto mainline is needed for a clean merge, do it.

## Rules for this work

- **Verify every finding against the code before changing anything.** A finding may be WRONG. If one is wrong, reply on the PR thread with the evidence and leave the code alone — never make a bogus change to silence a bad finding.
- **Add tests for every behavioral fix** — the NaN guard, the DST rounding, and both schema constraints. Tests live alongside source: `packages/*/src/**/*.test.ts`.
- **Renderer test fixtures must place the box at a non-zero `y`** — `y: 0` has previously masked a real height/overdraw bug in this repo. If you touch or add a holidays renderer test, use a non-zero `y`.
- Project constraints: **ES2020 target** (no `new Error(msg, { cause })` or other ES2022+), strict mode with `noUncheckedIndexedAccess`, `verbatimModuleSyntax` (use `import type`), and **relative imports inside `core`/`data`/`renderer`/`api` MUST carry `.js` extensions**. Cross-package imports use the bare specifier (`@infobento/renderer`), never a subpath.

## Quality gate (there is no `npm run gate` or `test:all` in this repo)

    npm run build && npm test && npm run lint && npm run format:check

Run from the clone root before pushing. If `format:check` flags files you did not touch, local prettier likely lags the package-lock pin — run `npm install` to sync rather than reformatting those files. If a type error appears in a downstream package, rebuild the shared package first before re-running typecheck.

## Ship it

    git push origin HEAD:rfc3-223

The review workflow (`claude-review.yml`) only runs on `labeled` events, so a push alone will NOT re-trigger review. Cycle the label:

    gh pr edit 226 --repo $REPO --remove-label review-requested
    sleep 3
    gh pr edit 226 --repo $REPO --add-label review-requested

Then wait:

    gh pr checks 226 --repo $REPO --watch

Wrap `gh pr checks` in a retry loop if it errors (up to 3 attempts, 30s apart). If the PR has merge conflicts the review check reports SKIPPED — rebase onto `origin/mainline`, push, then cycle the label again.

## Merge criteria

- Cap at **3 review-requested cycles**, OR stop earlier when the `🔍 Review verdict: N findings` count stops dropping across 2 cycles AND a sample of inline comments is nits-only (`Nit` / `Minor concern` / `(not blocking)`).
- Before merging, confirm no outstanding `CHANGES_REQUESTED`:

      gh api repos/$REPO/pulls/226/reviews --jq '[.[] | select(.state=="CHANGES_REQUESTED")] | length'

  Group by author, take each reviewer's LATEST state.
- Do NOT merge while any 🔴 finding is unresolved, regardless of cycle count.

Then:

    gh pr merge 226 --repo $REPO --squash --delete-branch

After merging, close linked issues GitHub did not auto-close:

    LINKED=$(gh pr view 226 --repo $REPO --json body --jq '.body' | grep -oE '(Closes|Fixes|Resolves) #[0-9]+' | grep -oE '[0-9]+')
    for I in $LINKED; do
      S=$(gh issue view $I --repo $REPO --json state --jq '.state')
      [ "$S" != "CLOSED" ] && gh issue close $I --repo $REPO --reason completed --comment "Closed by merged PR #226."
    done

## Report

When done, state plainly: which findings you fixed, which you rejected and why, what tests you added, how many review cycles ran, the final verdict count, and whether the PR merged. If you could not merge, say exactly what blocked it.
